WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:05.520
They say you should always prepare for the&nbsp;
worst, but it turns out that these beloved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:09.680
actors didn't have to. From horror&nbsp;
classics to historical epics, these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:15.240
hit movies featured stars who weren't exactly&nbsp;
optimistic about their work during production.

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:20.240
"Boogie Nights" was seen by many as a comeback&nbsp;
movie for Burt Reynolds. The peak of his fame&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:24.320
was already long behind him, and he had&nbsp;
spent much of the '90s in forgettable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:28.600
if not outright embarrassing films. For&nbsp;
the first time in a good number of years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:31.920
he was getting praise for his acting,&nbsp;
and he would go on to win a Golden&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:37.274
Globe and be nominated for an Oscar for his&nbsp;
performance as adult filmmaker Jack Horner.

00:00:37.274 --> 00:00:41.000
Surely Reynolds appreciated what&nbsp;
"Boogie Nights" did for his career,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:47.600
right? Not even close. Reynolds famously bashed&nbsp;
the film every chance he got and continued to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:54.160
express regret for his involvement as recently as&nbsp;
2015 — 18 years after the movie's release and just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:58.800
three years before his death. The veteran actor&nbsp;
claimed that he didn't even want to do the movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:04.160
in the first place, and said that he had turned&nbsp;
it down seven times before finally giving in.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:09.000
Reynolds also said that he hated working with&nbsp;
writer and director Paul Thomas Anderson — so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:13.120
much so that he had to be physically&nbsp;
held back from assaulting the filmmaker.

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:17.160
If that wasn't enough, Reynolds just&nbsp;
wasn't impressed with the movie or its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:20.800
subject matter — and he didn't see&nbsp;
how anyone else possibly could be.

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:24.000
 
"You wanted to hit the director, Paul Thomas Anderson, you wanted to hit him in the face."

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:27.703
 
"Uh, no, I don't want to hit him in the face. I just wanted to hit him."

00:01:27.816 --> 00:01:35.440
The 2007 movie "300" was arguably the breakout&nbsp;
project for both its director and its lead actor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:40.480
Zack Snyder had received acclaim for his "Dawn&nbsp;
of the Dead" remake, but it was with "300" that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:45.640
he truly established the filmmaking style that&nbsp;
would follow him for much of his career to come.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.640 --> 00:01:52.040
As for Gerard Butler, Leonidas was far from his&nbsp;
first film role, but it was his first huge hit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.040 --> 00:01:56.960
as the top-billed player. When looking back at the&nbsp;
film in an interview with The Hollywood Reporter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.960 --> 00:02:01.360
Butler revealed that he and Snyder connected&nbsp;
immediately during their first meeting, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:06.800
were mutually excited to work together. But once&nbsp;
the actor got on set, it was a different story.

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:11.640
"300" was filmed entirely in front of&nbsp;
bluescreens, with its backgrounds filled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:16.560
in digitally during post-production — something&nbsp;
much more common now than it was at the time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:21.000
It was certainly a new experience for Butler,&nbsp;
and he had a lot of trouble envisioning what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:25.720
the final product would actually look like as&nbsp;
a result. While watching an actor reacting to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.720 --> 00:02:29.880
a burning village while simply pointing at&nbsp;
a bluescreen, he couldn't help but think —

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:32.560
"Oh my God. This movie's going to suck."

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:35.960
Once Butler saw the finished film&nbsp;
with the added effects, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.960 --> 00:02:41.040
he was blown away — and thankfully for&nbsp;
both him and Snyder, audiences were too.

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:46.480
"Friday the 13th" bad guy Jason Voorhees&nbsp;
has become an icon of the horror genre,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:51.800
the hockey mask-covered face of the long-running&nbsp;
franchise. He's appeared in everything from comics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:56.800
to a Mortal Kombat game, but he wasn't the bad&nbsp;
guy in the first "Friday." Most of the killing in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.800 --> 00:03:02.880
the 1980 slasher movie was done by Jason's mother&nbsp;
Pamela, who was played by the late Betsy Palmer.

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:07.000
Palmer once admitted that the only reason&nbsp;
she appeared in the film was because she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:11.800
needed a new car — and she had her eye on a&nbsp;
Volkswagen Scirocco. As soon as she realized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:16.080
that she would be portraying the villain in a&nbsp;
low-budget horror flick, Palmer assumed that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:20.680
the happenings at Camp Crystal Lake would come&nbsp;
and go without much fanfare. As she admitted —

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:25.320
"So the script came and I read it and&nbsp;
said, 'What a piece of [s---]. No one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:30.120
is ever going to see this. It will come. And&nbsp;
it will go. And I will have my Scirocco.'"

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:32.960
Instead, "Friday the 13th" was a smash hit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:37.160
turning Pamela Voorhees and&nbsp;
her son into horror icons.

00:03:37.160 --> 00:03:41.104
"Don't let her get away,&nbsp;
mommy. Don't let her live!”

00:03:41.104 --> 00:03:45.160
“I won't, Jason. I won't."

00:03:45.160 --> 00:03:48.960
Tommy Lee Jones is known for playing&nbsp;
no-nonsense characters that aren't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:52.200
afraid to hurt feelings — and if&nbsp;
his co-stars are to be believed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:57.160
Jones himself has a similar reputation. From&nbsp;
his disdain of Jim Carrey on the set of "Batman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.160 --> 00:04:01.480
Forever" to his clashes with director Barry&nbsp;
Sonnenfeld during the production of "Men in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.480 --> 00:04:06.880
Black," there might be a good reason why Jones&nbsp;
is frequently typecast as a crotchety grump.

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:11.400
Even while working on the movie that earned&nbsp;
Jones his only Academy Award thus far,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:16.160
"The Fugitive," Jones still didn't seem all&nbsp;
that happy to be there. He also didn't seem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:20.440
to think the movie was any good. In a&nbsp;
Rolling Stone oral history of the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.440 --> 00:04:25.080
Jones recalled a moment when he was filming&nbsp;
a scene and things just weren't falling into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:29.440
place — so he immediately assumed that the&nbsp;
worst was going to happen. As he put it —

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:33.000
"I remember thinking in the back of&nbsp;
my mind, 'I'll never work again. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:37.840
is never going to work.' And the best&nbsp;
thing I can do is be as clear, concise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:42.440
and coherent as possible, deliver these&nbsp;
lines as cleanly and dutifully as possible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.440 --> 00:04:46.160
and maybe I’ll get another job&nbsp;
one day, somewhere down the line."

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:50.320
Like many people in Hollywood, Chris Pratt&nbsp;
was hesitant to believe the first "Guardians&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:55.880
of the Galaxy" movie could succeed. After&nbsp;
all, the Guardians were a C-list squad with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.880 --> 00:05:01.040
far less name recognition than the Avengers&nbsp;
and X-Men. Both Pratt and director James Gunn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:04.960
admitted that they were both worried&nbsp;
that their careers would be over if,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:09.280
as some pundits predicted, the film&nbsp;
turned out to be "Marvel's first bomb."

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:13.840
Pratt in particular was amazed that&nbsp;
Marvel even opted to fund the project,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:15.800
remarking about Hollywood executives —

00:05:15.800 --> 00:05:18.640
"Everyone wants to win big,&nbsp;
but no one wants to gamble."

00:05:18.640 --> 00:05:19.800
The actor continued —

00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:23.720
"Every rule said, 'You don’t make&nbsp;
a movie with a talking tree,' 'You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.720 --> 00:05:26.901
don’t spend this much money on&nbsp;
a raccoon with a gun.' For me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.901 --> 00:05:32.120
I was like, 'Oh, so this movie is going to&nbsp;
bomb. Done. This is the end of my career.'"

00:05:32.120 --> 00:05:34.600
"There's a little pee coming out of me right now."

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:40.480
More than $700 million and heaps of critical&nbsp;
praise later, the Guardians were household names,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.480 --> 00:05:46.360
Pratt was the face of a franchise, and Gunn was&nbsp;
one of Hollywood's hottest writer-directors.

00:05:46.360 --> 00:05:51.000
It may have come out two decades ago, but&nbsp;
"Elf" is still one of the newer films to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:55.960
be added to the list of undisputed Christmas&nbsp;
classics. Directed by Jon Favreau before his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.960 --> 00:06:01.000
jump to Marvel and "Star Wars," the movie&nbsp;
stars Will Ferrell as Buddy, a human man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:06.280
who grew up among Santa's toy-making elves.&nbsp;
After he learns the truth about his existence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.280 --> 00:06:11.640
Buddy makes his way to America — and he gets&nbsp;
into some pretty wacky hijinks once he arrives.

00:06:11.640 --> 00:06:17.240
Will Ferrell is one of the most famous comedians&nbsp;
around nowadays — but in 2003, he had only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.240 --> 00:06:22.080
recently left "Saturday Night Live" and was still&nbsp;
beefing up his filmography. He still had a famous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.080 --> 00:06:27.120
face, though. While filming the outdoor scenes for&nbsp;
"Elf" in New York City, the cameras were hidden so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:32.200
that Ferrell could get some real reactions out of&nbsp;
real people. Unfortunately, some of those people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.200 --> 00:06:37.520
recognized him — and as a result, they expressed&nbsp;
concern over the fact that he was walking around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.520 --> 00:06:43.320
in an elf costume and asked if he was doing okay.&nbsp;
The experience made the actor question whether the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.320 --> 00:06:48.120
movie was going to work on the big screen, and he&nbsp;
worried that it was going to effectively end his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.120 --> 00:06:53.040
movie career just as it was beginning. Obviously,&nbsp;
the movie ended up resonating with a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.040 --> 00:06:58.480
people, and "Elf" was a hit in its day before it&nbsp;
became an enduring classic of the holiday season.

00:06:58.480 --> 00:07:04.240
Richard Dreyfuss was fortunate enough to star in&nbsp;
several undeniably classic films fairly early in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.240 --> 00:07:10.160
his career, as he ended up on the radars of future&nbsp;
icons George Lucas and Steven Spielberg early in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.160 --> 00:07:14.680
their respective Hollywood journeys. "American&nbsp;
Graffiti" ended up being Dreyfuss's first big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.680 --> 00:07:19.640
role, and he added both "Jaws" and "Close&nbsp;
Encounters of the Third Kind" to his resume&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.640 --> 00:07:25.440
before the end of the 1970s. With "Jaws," Dreyfuss&nbsp;
got to be part of the movie that is often said to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.440 --> 00:07:30.640
have invented the very concept of the summer&nbsp;
blockbuster — and everyone involved seemed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.640 --> 00:07:34.480
understand that it was something special.&nbsp;
Everyone, it seems, except for Dreyfuss.

00:07:34.480 --> 00:07:39.320
In a career retrospective he did for Yahoo,&nbsp;
Dreyfuss said of the vibe on the "Jaws" set —

00:07:39.320 --> 00:07:42.120
"Everyone had thought they&nbsp;
had struck gold, and I said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.120 --> 00:07:46.160
'What are you talking about? It's just a&nbsp;
little movie.' So when the film was released,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.160 --> 00:07:50.880
I found myself going back to the talk shows and&nbsp;
saying 'I'm the guy who didn't believe in it.'"

00:07:50.880 --> 00:07:54.440
He couldn't have been more wrong.&nbsp;
"Jaws" was so big that it set new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.440 --> 00:07:57.760
standards for what it means for a movie to be big.

00:07:57.760 --> 00:08:01.840
Bill Murray voicing the main&nbsp;
character in 2004's "Garfield:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.840 --> 00:08:08.760
The Movie" felt like a full-circle moment for&nbsp;
many fans. Garfield's voice from 1982 to 2001&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.760 --> 00:08:13.720
was an actor named Lorenzo Music, and he&nbsp;
also played Peter Venkman in the cartoon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.720 --> 00:08:19.160
"The Real Ghostbusters." Since Music died&nbsp;
in 2001, he couldn't voice the live-action&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.160 --> 00:08:23.800
Garfield — so who better to step in than&nbsp;
the original live-action Peter Venkman?

00:08:23.800 --> 00:08:29.080
Still, nostalgia wasn't the reason Murray signed&nbsp;
on to voice everyone's favorite lasagna-loving fat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.080 --> 00:08:34.240
cat. When he saw that the movie was co-written&nbsp;
by Joel Cohen, he jumped at the chance to work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.240 --> 00:08:39.480
with one of the esteemed Coen brothers, who proved&nbsp;
their comedy chops with "Raising Arizona" and "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.480 --> 00:08:45.000
Big Lebowski." As Murray was recording his lines,&nbsp;
he was shocked at how bad he thought they were,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:48.960
but he stuck with it and tried to make the&nbsp;
best of it. When things didn't improve,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:53.920
he asked to watch back the scenes that had already&nbsp;
been cut together — and he remarked out loud —

00:08:53.920 --> 00:08:57.960
"Who the hell cut this thing? Who did&nbsp;
this? What the f--- was Coen thinking?"

00:08:57.960 --> 00:09:02.360
That's when someone informed Murray that it&nbsp;
wasn't the Oscar winner Joel Coen; COEN, who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.360 --> 00:09:07.840
had worked on "Garfield," but Joel Cohen;&nbsp;
COHEN, with an “H”. At that point he was too&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.840 --> 00:09:13.000
deep to back out, so he finished the job&nbsp;
on what ended up being a box office hit.

00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:18.160
"So do you have many regrets?"

00:09:18.160 --> 00:09:20.720
"Garfield, maybe."

00:09:20.720 --> 00:09:26.080
Australian actor Geoffrey Rush knows his way&nbsp;
around an Oscar-caliber performance — he's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.080 --> 00:09:30.560
delivered four so far, winning for "Shine"&nbsp;
and receiving nominations for "Shakespeare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.560 --> 00:09:35.440
in Love," "Quills," and "The King's Speech."&nbsp;
That doesn't mean he always knows going into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.440 --> 00:09:40.120
a project that it's going to be acclaimed, let&nbsp;
alone that anyone is even going to be bothered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.120 --> 00:09:44.880
to see the movie at all. He had serious doubts&nbsp;
about one of those very projects — and he ended&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.880 --> 00:09:50.360
up eating his words. Despite his prediction,&nbsp;
"The King's Speech" was an undisputed success.

00:09:50.360 --> 00:09:53.920
Rush reminisced on the moment he&nbsp;
first received the script for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.920 --> 00:09:58.880
historical drama about King George VI's&nbsp;
struggles with stuttering, recalling —

00:09:58.880 --> 00:10:02.640
"When we sat and read the initial&nbsp;
script [...] the first thing I read was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.640 --> 00:10:07.880
'Well no one will want to go and see this,'&nbsp;
let alone, 'Oh this is an Oscar-worthy film.'"

00:10:07.880 --> 00:10:11.880
With that in mind, Rush found it rather&nbsp;
funny when people accused the movie of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.880 --> 00:10:15.840
being so-called "Oscar bait." But&nbsp;
whether or not that was the case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.840 --> 00:10:21.000
it snagged an impressive 12 nominations and&nbsp;
won four, including Best Picture. As for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.000 --> 00:10:25.800
Rush's assumption that nobody would go to see it&nbsp;
— "The King's Speech" was a surprisingly massive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.800 --> 00:10:32.080
box office hit for a movie of its kind, netting&nbsp;
over $400 million at the worldwide box office.

00:10:32.080 --> 00:10:35.840
It's no big secret that Robert Pattinson&nbsp;
was never entirely comfortable with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.840 --> 00:10:39.560
the level of fame that starring in&nbsp;
"Twilight" brought him. Thankfully,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.560 --> 00:10:43.600
he's done more than enough acclaimed work&nbsp;
since then to successfully distance himself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.600 --> 00:10:48.320
from the five-movie franchise — and nowadays,&nbsp;
he's more commonly associated with films like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.320 --> 00:10:52.400
"The Batman" and "The Lighthouse." But&nbsp;
why would Pattinson take on the role of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.400 --> 00:10:57.760
the famously sexy vampire Edward Cullen if he&nbsp;
didn't want to be a globally famous heartthrob?

00:10:57.760 --> 00:11:00.840
It turns out that Pattinson didn't&nbsp;
think "Twilight" was going to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.840 --> 00:11:04.600
that big at all when he auditioned and&nbsp;
eventually accepted the role. Even though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.600 --> 00:11:09.640
Stephanie Meyer's urban fantasy saga was an&nbsp;
unavoidable phenomenon in the United States,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.640 --> 00:11:14.480
it was apparently much less popular in Pattinson's&nbsp;
native London. He claimed that when he tried to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.480 --> 00:11:18.840
buy the books for research after he was cast in&nbsp;
the first movie, he couldn't find any stores in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.840 --> 00:11:23.080
the city that even sold them. He also stated&nbsp;
that among his circle of friends and family,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.080 --> 00:11:27.760
he only knew one person who had read the books&nbsp;
— a friend of his sister's. Given all that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.760 --> 00:11:32.280
along with the fact that director Catherine&nbsp;
Hardwicke had only done relatively small films&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.280 --> 00:11:37.720
up to that point, Pattinson had no reason to&nbsp;
expect "Twilight" to make much of an impact.

00:11:37.720 --> 00:11:43.240
Even though Jackie Chan was an international&nbsp;
action movie superstar dating back to the 1970s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.240 --> 00:11:46.320
he struggled for years to fully&nbsp;
break into Hollywood. It wasn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.320 --> 00:11:51.280
until "Rumble in the Bronx" made it to&nbsp;
the States in 1996 that Chan finally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.280 --> 00:11:55.680
got the crossover hit that had eluded him&nbsp;
for so long. Two years later, he starred&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.680 --> 00:12:01.000
in the American-made buddy cop action comedy&nbsp;
"Rush Hour" alongside comedian Chris Tucker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.000 --> 00:12:05.840
and it ended up being the biggest worldwide box&nbsp;
office hit of Chan's career up to that point.

00:12:05.840 --> 00:12:09.240
While filming "Rush Hour," though,&nbsp;
Chan didn't think it was working. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.240 --> 00:12:13.320
was frustrated about not being given more&nbsp;
creative control over his fight scenes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.320 --> 00:12:17.880
and he had concerns about the language&nbsp;
barrier between himself and Tucker.

00:12:17.880 --> 00:12:24.680
"Do you understand the words&nbsp;
that are coming out of my mouth?"

00:12:24.680 --> 00:12:29.080
Chan had no faith in "Rush Hour" at&nbsp;
all — so much so that, even when the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.080 --> 00:12:33.160
audience at the premiere was loving it,&nbsp;
Chan told Yahoo that he was thinking —

00:12:33.160 --> 00:12:35.560
"Why are they laughing, I just don't understand."

00:12:35.560 --> 00:12:39.680
The fact that "Rush Hour" tanked in his&nbsp;
native China only seemed to reinforce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.680 --> 00:12:43.800
Chan's feelings on the movie's quality&nbsp;
— but it was an undeniably huge hit in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.800 --> 00:12:48.640
the U.S. Its sequel did even better at&nbsp;
both the U.S. and worldwide box offices,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.640 --> 00:12:51.800
even though Chan thought it was&nbsp;
even worse than the original.

00:12:51.800 --> 00:12:57.520
James Marsden has had an interesting and varied&nbsp;
career, one that includes comedies, dramas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.520 --> 00:13:03.400
video game adaptations, musicals, superhero&nbsp;
movies, and more. He's also done his fair share&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.400 --> 00:13:08.640
of romance films, including one that's especially&nbsp;
iconic among fans of the genre — "The Notebook,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.640 --> 00:13:14.520
the 2004 Nicolas Sparks adaptation that stars&nbsp;
Ryan Gosling and Rachel McAdams in the lead roles.

00:13:14.520 --> 00:13:20.560
Marsden played Lon Hammond, the guy who McAdams's&nbsp;
Allie ends up with after she thinks Gosling's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.560 --> 00:13:25.240
Noah has stopped writing to her — and she later&nbsp;
cheats on him with Noah when they reconnect. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.240 --> 00:13:29.800
all pretty standard Nicolas Sparks fare, which&nbsp;
is exactly what Marsden worried about while he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.800 --> 00:13:34.760
was making the movie. In an interview with Out&nbsp;
magazine looking back at his career, he joked —

00:13:34.760 --> 00:13:39.120
"There are some movies that I've been in&nbsp;
that I was sure people would laugh at,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.120 --> 00:13:42.920
that have become huge. I thought 'The&nbsp;
Notebook' was going to be a schmaltzy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.920 --> 00:13:45.800
Movie of the Week-type thing, and here we are!"

00:13:45.800 --> 00:13:50.280
Whether "The Notebook" is schmaltzy or not&nbsp;
is a matter of opinion — but as far as money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.280 --> 00:13:56.160
is concerned, the raw data speaks for itself. The&nbsp;
movie grossed over $100 million at the box office,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.160 --> 00:14:00.360
and it remains the best-reviewed&nbsp;
Nicolas Sparks adaptation to date.

00:14:00.360 --> 00:14:04.600
It has since been reappraised in a&nbsp;
less positive light by a lot of people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.600 --> 00:14:10.400
but "Forrest Gump" was a crowd-pleasing hit&nbsp;
when it came out in 1994. Star Tom Hanks even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.400 --> 00:14:15.000
saw fit to come out and defend the movie against&nbsp;
its detractors, saying that it's only because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:15.000 --> 00:14:19.640
the movie was such a big hit that people have&nbsp;
seen fit to take shots at it in recent years.

00:14:19.640 --> 00:14:23.840
It's interesting to hear Hanks going to bat&nbsp;
for "Forrest Gump" now, considering that he had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.840 --> 00:14:28.400
moments in which he wasn't especially confident in&nbsp;
the movie while the movie was being filmed. During&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.400 --> 00:14:34.240
the 2023 New Yorker Live event, Hanks admitted&nbsp;
that he questioned director Robert Zemeckis on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.240 --> 00:14:40.080
whether anyone was really going to care about the&nbsp;
movie or its lead character. He asked Zemeckis —

00:14:40.080 --> 00:14:43.920
"This guy sitting on a thing in these&nbsp;
goofy shoes and this cuckoo suit with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:43.920 --> 00:14:46.680
a suitcase full of 'Curious&nbsp;
George' books and stuff like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.680 --> 00:14:50.640
that. Are we doing anything here that&nbsp;
is going to make any sense to anybody?"

00:14:50.640 --> 00:14:54.040
Hanks claims that Zemeckis did&nbsp;
little to reassure him. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:54.040 --> 00:14:56.160
the director supposedly responded with —

00:14:56.160 --> 00:14:58.869
"We may be sowing the seeds&nbsp;
of our own destruction."

00:14:59.800 --> 00:15:03.920
"What's my destiny, mama?"

00:15:03.920 --> 00:15:06.840
"You're gonna have to figure&nbsp;
that out for yourself."

00:15:06.840 --> 00:15:12.240
It all worked out fine — including Best Actor&nbsp;
and Best Director Oscar wins for the duo.

00:15:12.240 --> 00:15:17.720
Beyond a few cameos, Rita Moreno was the only&nbsp;
member of the original "West Side Story" movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:17.720 --> 00:15:23.760
cast to play a major role in the 2021 Steven&nbsp;
Spielberg-directed remake. In the 1961 version,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.760 --> 00:15:28.400
she was Anita, while in the 2021 film,&nbsp;
she played an entirely new character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:28.400 --> 00:15:33.280
named Valentina. Her connection to both films&nbsp;
makes her an important part of the overall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:33.280 --> 00:15:37.240
"West Side Story" legacy — which makes it all&nbsp;
the more surprising that she didn't think the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:37.240 --> 00:15:41.960
original movie, directed by Robert Wise and&nbsp;
Jerome Robbins, was going to be a success.

00:15:41.960 --> 00:15:46.000
In a 2021 appearance on "60&nbsp;
Minutes," Moreno admitted flat out —

00:15:46.000 --> 00:15:48.520
"I didn't even think the movie&nbsp;
was going [to] do that well."

00:15:48.520 --> 00:15:53.040
She went on to explain that she pulled her&nbsp;
co-star George Chakiris aside on the set one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:53.040 --> 00:15:57.200
day and prepared him for the worst. She&nbsp;
said nobody was going to want to see the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:57.200 --> 00:16:03.040
movie because it lacked fancy costuming, had very&nbsp;
operatic singing at times, and was about gangs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:03.040 --> 00:16:07.920
that danced. Moreno made sure to point out that&nbsp;
she found the movie to be brilliant — it's just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:07.920 --> 00:16:12.560
that she didn't think it would connect with&nbsp;
moviegoing audiences. But it very much did,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:12.560 --> 00:16:17.160
and it won her an Oscar — which was her&nbsp;
first step toward her eventual EGOT.

00:16:17.160 --> 00:16:21.000
Anyone who worked on the first couple of&nbsp;
entries of the Marvel Cinematic Universe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:21.000 --> 00:16:25.720
could be forgiven for being unsure if they were&nbsp;
making a hit — but when "The Avengers" broke&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:25.720 --> 00:16:31.320
the billion-dollar mark in 2012, there was no&nbsp;
denying that the franchise was a massive success.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:31.320 --> 00:16:37.320
Hype only grew for the MCU — and when "Avengers:&nbsp;
Endgame" arrived in theaters seven years later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:37.320 --> 00:16:41.600
it was less of a film release and more of&nbsp;
a worldwide cultural event. Considering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:41.600 --> 00:16:46.040
that it became the highest grossing film&nbsp;
ever at the time in just three months,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:46.040 --> 00:16:50.320
it's hard to imagine that anyone was&nbsp;
pessimistic about its chances for success.

00:16:50.320 --> 00:16:55.160
And yet, Scarlet Witch actor Elizabeth Olsen&nbsp;
worried about that very thing regarding the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:55.160 --> 00:17:00.480
epic climax of the MCU's first three phases.&nbsp;
During a late night interview with Jimmy Fallon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:00.480 --> 00:17:03.400
she admitted that it's difficult&nbsp;
for her to watch herself in new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:03.400 --> 00:17:07.320
Marvel films because she always&nbsp;
assumes that they'll do poorly.

00:17:07.320 --> 00:17:11.600
"I saw 'Avengers: Endgame,' and I looked&nbsp;
around me and I said, 'Is it our first flop?'"

00:17:11.600 --> 00:17:13.626
"'Avengers: Endgame' is one of the biggest&nbsp;
movies in the history of the world."

00:17:13.626 --> 00:17:15.080
"Yeah. Yeah."

00:17:15.080 --> 00:17:19.800
Only several movies in the MCU have disappointed&nbsp;
at the box office, and Elizabeth Olsen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:19.800 --> 00:17:25.240
hasn't appeared in any of them. It seems that&nbsp;
audiences always turn out for the Scarlet Witch.

00:17:25.240 --> 00:17:30.040
With near-universal acclaim and hundreds of&nbsp;
millions of dollars made at the box office,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:30.040 --> 00:17:34.000
"It" ranks among the most critically and&nbsp;
commercially successful horror movies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:34.000 --> 00:17:38.760
ever made. That came as a huge relief to&nbsp;
actor Bill Skarsgård, who was convinced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:38.760 --> 00:17:42.840
something would go wrong during production.&nbsp;
He admitted in an interview with Variety —

00:17:42.840 --> 00:17:44.840
"I just felt like something&nbsp;
really terrible was going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:44.840 --> 00:17:48.360
happen to me personally — things&nbsp;
just seemed to be going too well."

00:17:48.360 --> 00:17:53.160
Even when the film was projected to wildly&nbsp;
surpass the studio's most conservative estimates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:53.160 --> 00:17:58.920
of success based on early reviews, Skarsgård still&nbsp;
apparently managed to convince himself something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:58.920 --> 00:18:03.200
would come along and undo it all. Nothing&nbsp;
particularly awful ended up happening — and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:03.200 --> 00:18:08.360
even though "It: Chapter 2" didn't quite live up&nbsp;
to the expectations set by the first film, the two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:08.360 --> 00:18:13.840
movies brought in over a billion dollars in total.&nbsp;
Now all that's left is for the world to decide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:13.840 --> 00:18:19.920
whether Skarsgård is a scary-looking hot guy, a&nbsp;
hot-looking scary guy, or something in between.

00:18:19.920 --> 00:18:23.400
If you had to guess who in the cast&nbsp;
of "Home Alone" was skeptical of its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:23.400 --> 00:18:27.320
potential to be a hit — or even a decent&nbsp;
movie at all — it would be understandable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:27.320 --> 00:18:30.640
to assume that the person in question&nbsp;
was Joe Pesci. While there has been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:30.640 --> 00:18:35.000
some anecdotal evidence to suggest&nbsp;
that to be the case, it was actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:35.000 --> 00:18:40.640
John Heard, the actor behind patriarch Peter&nbsp;
McCallister, who loudly expressed his doubts.

00:18:40.640 --> 00:18:45.360
While appearing on the podcast "Here's the Thing&nbsp;
with Alec Baldwin," director Chris Columbus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:45.360 --> 00:18:50.080
explained that John Heard didn't seem to think&nbsp;
that "Home Alone" was any good during production,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:50.080 --> 00:18:54.880
and he made sure that everyone around him knew it.&nbsp;
Once the final cut of the film premiered, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:54.880 --> 00:18:59.840
Heard had the same reaction as the majority of&nbsp;
viewers and greatly enjoyed watching his onscreen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:59.840 --> 00:19:04.160
son defend his home from the Wet Bandits.&nbsp;
Columbus claimed that during the production&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:04.160 --> 00:19:09.760
of "Home Alone 2: Lost in New York," Heard broke&nbsp;
character in the middle of a scene to declare —

00:19:09.760 --> 00:19:12.360
"I just would like to say to Chris and the crew,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:12.360 --> 00:19:17.800
I owe you a big apology. We made a great movie&nbsp;
the first time and I'm here to support you."

00:19:17.800 --> 00:19:23.120
Edie Falco didn't exactly think that "Avatar:&nbsp;
The Way of Water" was going to fail — she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:23.120 --> 00:19:27.720
thought that it had already failed. As one of&nbsp;
the most ambitious sci-fi films of all time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:27.720 --> 00:19:32.560
James Cameron's 2022 sequel needed a&nbsp;
whole lot of post-production to properly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:32.560 --> 00:19:36.760
transport viewers to Pandora. Editing&nbsp;
and effects took years to complete,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:36.760 --> 00:19:41.640
so actors had to be captured on camera quite&nbsp;
a while before the movie was released. It took&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:41.640 --> 00:19:46.600
long enough that Falco assumed "The Way of&nbsp;
Water" — in which she plays General Frances&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:46.600 --> 00:19:51.040
Ardmore — had already come out, flopped&nbsp;
at the box office, and been forgotten.

00:19:51.040 --> 00:19:55.800
During a 2023 appearance on "The View,"&nbsp;
Falco explained that she filmed her scenes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:55.800 --> 00:20:00.880
for "Avatar 2" four years earlier and moved&nbsp;
on to other projects. Since she hadn't heard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:00.880 --> 00:20:05.600
anything about it for a while, she assumed that&nbsp;
it had simply come and gone without much fanfare.

00:20:05.600 --> 00:20:08.440
"Somebody mentioned 'Avatar' and I&nbsp;
thought, 'Oh I guess it came out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:08.440 --> 00:20:11.640
didn't do very well,' cause I didn't hear&nbsp;
anything so— but it happens, you know."

00:20:11.640 --> 00:20:15.600
It was only when excitement ramped up for&nbsp;
"The Way of Water" that Falco realized it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:15.600 --> 00:20:20.320
hadn't hit theaters yet — but she was happy to&nbsp;
learn that it hadn't been a failure after all.

00:20:20.320 --> 00:20:25.240
Nobody could have predicted back in 1939 that&nbsp;
"Gone with the Wind" was going to be discussed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:25.240 --> 00:20:30.000
and held aloft as an all-time classic&nbsp;
film nearly a century later. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:34.160
the people making it must have at least known&nbsp;
they were making a good movie, right? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:34.160 --> 00:20:38.760
certainly some of the cast and crew did —&nbsp;
but that list doesn't include Vivien Leigh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:38.760 --> 00:20:42.000
who played Scarlett O'Hara in&nbsp;
the historical romance epic.

00:20:42.000 --> 00:20:46.440
In letters discovered by The Hollywood Reporter&nbsp;
between Leigh and her second husband Laurence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:46.440 --> 00:20:51.000
Olivier, neither actor had high hopes for "Gone&nbsp;
with the Wind" — and Olivier even worried about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:51.000 --> 00:20:55.560
the damage it was going to do to Leigh's&nbsp;
still-rising career. Olivier wrote to Leigh —

00:20:55.560 --> 00:20:59.000
"You have got to justify yourself&nbsp;
in the next two or three films (or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:59.000 --> 00:21:03.120
even two or three years) by proving that the&nbsp;
presumable failure of ['Gone with the Wind']&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:03.120 --> 00:21:07.680
was not your fault and you can only do that&nbsp;
by being really good in the following parts."

00:21:07.680 --> 00:21:12.840
Before a single review was released in the papers,&nbsp;
the couple were already planning Leigh's career&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:12.840 --> 00:21:17.840
comeback after how badly "Gone with the Wind"&nbsp;
was apparently going to humiliate her. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:17.840 --> 00:21:21.120
she ended up winning her first of&nbsp;
two Oscars for her work in the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:21.120 --> 00:21:28.040
which is the highest-grossing movie of all time by&nbsp;
a fairly wide margin if you adjust for inflation.

